  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure continues overhead tonight, with calm winds \nexpected. Dry air through the column will also promote a mostly \nclear night sky, leading to excellent radiational cooling \nconditions.\n\nOvernight lows could dip a few degrees lower than Friday night \namid these conditions. This would again bring 40s to the \nmountains and northern valleys, with lower 50s across the \ninterior and coast. \n\nSunday, the high will be exiting east with return flow improving\nmoisture advection in the low levels with high clouds spilling\nin from the west. A mid deck of clouds will then increase SW to\nNE in the afternoon ahead of the next weather system bringing \nrain Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure advances along the US/CAN border towards New \nEngland Sunday night into early Monday morning. The triple point\nalong a frontal system will tap into an area of southern \nmoisture, creating a period of showers and more stratiform rain \nafter midnight. \n\nSome showers will begin through the evening for western NH, but\nshowers areawide should hold off until at least sunset. \n\nThe greatest rainfall rates look to pass through the area west \nto east late Sunday night into early/mid Monday morning. This is\nwhere above normal PWATs, a deep warm cloud layer, and forcing \ncoincide to create a period of enhanced rainfall. Global \nensembles seem to be in more agreement this morning, but have \nshifted the QPF max a bit further north across the mountains of\nnorthern NH and western ME. There could be some localized areas\nthat see an inch of rain before sunrise Monday, but the lack of\ngreater instability will isolate these to areas that see \nrainfall over a good portion of the overnight.\n\nRemaining moisture lingers without much flow behind the \ndisturbance, and this could lead to an expansive region of \ndrizzle or light rain showers through the day Monday. \nRegardless, low clouds likely last through the afternoon.\n\nRainfall totals for the mountains and foothills of around 1 to \n1.5 inch is forecast, with a general 0.5 to 0.75 inch elsewhere.\nA good soaking rain event, if the forecast coverage comes to \nfruition. One potential failure mode is convection from a wave \nthrough the Mid Atlantic limiting how much deeper moisture makes\nit to New England. Regardless, rain will still be likely given \nthe low level jet dynamics and incoming front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA relatively zonal pattern looks to setup over the entire CONUS\nnext week. Low pressure over Canada will move eastward early \nnext week, allowing for the development of a cold front as it \ncrosses a region of upper- level divergence by Wednesday \nmorning. This cold front will move through New England Wednesday\nnight and could allow for the development of showers and \nthunderstorms along the front. \n\nWednesday also looks to be the warmest day of the week, with \ntemperatures in the low to mid-80s forecast. Despite the passage\nof this cold front, low pressure and some instability could \nlead to more scattered showers and thunderstorms towards the end\nof next week.\n\nThe second half of next week should also feature cooler \ntemperatures, with upper 70s and lower 80s in the forecast.",
